  "not_simulated": [
    {
      "what": "contrast between crisp and gone",
      "pins": ["V0"],
      "why": "The glass draws text at full contrast while VDD - V0 is at least 3.0 V (VLCD1, p. 51) and none below it, where the display on warns chip.protocol rule contrast. A real glass fades between the two, and darkens its unlit dots as V0 nears VSS.",
    },
    {
      "what": "blue transmissive visibility",
      "pins": ["A", "K"],
      "why": "On a blue negative transmissive 1602 LCD, characters are only visible when both the display and the backlight are on. `text` returns two 16-space blank lines while A - K is under the backlight's lit voltage.",
    },
    {
      "what": "enable rise and fall time",
      "pins": ["E"],
      "why": "The sheet's tEr and tEf, 20 ns maximum at 4.5 to 5.5 V (p. 52), are not checked. Cycle time, pulse width, set-up and hold are, from p. 52 at 4.5 V and above and p. 49 below.",
    },
    {
      "what": "input levels below 4.5 V",
      "why": "The inputs switch at 0.6 V and 2.2 V of a 5 V supply, as fractions of VDD. At 2.7 to 4.5 V the sheet's VIH1 is 0.7 VCC and VIL1 0.55 V (p. 48).",
    },
    {
      "what": "a busy flag held through a read",
      "why": "A read drives the busy flag and address as they stand tDDR after E rises, and holds them until E falls. The real busy flag can clear while E stays high.",
    },
    {
      "what": "5x10 font",
      "why": "A Function Set that selects one line of the 5x10 font (N = 0, F = 1) warns chip.protocol rule font. With two lines F is don't care (Table 8, p. 29). The glass always draws the 5x8 font, as a 16 x 2 glass has 8 rows to a character.",
    },
  ],
}
